+### 3. High: zero-bit sentinel behavior is undefined on GCC/Clang
 
-Known uncommitted tracked changes:
+`cpp/include/fracessa/bitset64.hpp:36-45` calls `__builtin_ctzll(0)`, which is
+undefined behavior. Nevertheless, `cpp/tests/test_bitset64.cpp:44-47` requires
+`find_pos_first_set_bit(0) == 64`, and `cpp/tests/test_bitset64.cpp:208-212`
+requires `lowest_set_bit_as_bit(0) == 0`. The latter also reaches a shift by 64.
+UBSan catches this immediately.
 
-```text
-cpp/CMakeLists.txt
-cpp/tests/CMakeLists.txt
-test.sh
-```
+Production call paths currently appear to maintain a nonzero invariant. To
+preserve hot-path speed, either make that unchecked precondition explicit and
+remove the contradictory zero-input API contract, or provide distinct checked
+boundary and unchecked hot-path helpers.
+
+The separate top-bit shift bug in `find_pos_next_set_bit(bits, 63)` is fixed in
+the current local working tree, but that fix is not committed to GitHub.
+
+### 4. High: `MPQueueRunner` can deadlock or wait forever
+
+The documented workflow in `python/wrapper_v1/mp.py:62-66` submits all work
+before reading results. Both queues are bounded (`:75-76`). Once the output
+queue fills, workers block at `:43`; the input queue can then fill and block the
+producer at `:101`.
+
+`python/wrapper_v1/mp.py:134-135` also waits forever if a native worker aborts
+before publishing its result. This requires worker-liveness detection, not a
+matrix computation timeout. Finally, `shutdown()` terminates workers after
+five seconds (`:137-147`), conflicting with the project's intentional
+unbounded matrix runtime.
+
+Required fix: expose one backpressure-safe streaming protocol, validate the
+number of submitted/results expected, detect dead worker exit codes while
+waiting, and do not terminate healthy long-running jobs as a normal shutdown
+policy.
+
+### 5. High: baseline regeneration can publish failed or incorrect baselines
+
+`python/verification/create_baselines.py:337-345` only prints ESS mismatches.
+It then writes both baseline files, prints "created successfully", and exits
+zero even when executions failed (`:355-408`). That can bless partial or known
+incorrect output as the correctness oracle.
+
+Required fix: write temporary files and atomically replace the baselines only
+after every matrix executes successfully and every independent expected ESS
+count agrees. Return nonzero on any mismatch or parse failure.
 
-Known untracked wrapper files:
+### 6. Medium: the safe parser accepts malformed dimension syntax
+
+`cpp/src/matrix_parser.cpp:40` calls `std::stoull` without checking how many
+characters were consumed, so a prefix such as `2x#...` is accepted as dimension
+2. The token loop also ignores a trailing comma. In addition,
+`cpp/src/pybind_module.cpp:59-80` classifies any parse failure with an in-range
+dimension as `INVALID_VALUE_COUNT`, including malformed rational tokens.
+
+Required fix: validate complete dimension consumption and delimiter grammar in
+the safe parser, and have the parser return the actual structured error instead
+of re-parsing input in pybind.
+
+### 7. Medium: generated run IDs can overwrite output
+
+`python/wrapper_v1/core.py:15-16` has only one-second resolution. Two runs with
+the same prefix in one second receive identical output paths from
+`python/wrapper_v1/sinks.py:77-107`, and sink constructors truncate files.
+
+Required fix: add a monotonic/UUID component or use exclusive file creation.
+
+### 8. Medium: legacy `Matrix` dimension auto-detection is incompatible with the CLI
+
+`python/fracessa_py.py:41-53` infers a square dimension from the number of
+values, but non-circular CLI matrices use `n*(n+1)/2` upper-triangular values.
+Valid three-value 2x2 input fails auto-detection, while four values are accepted
+by Python and then rejected by C++.
+
+Required fix: migrate the active benchmark to wrapper v1 and remove this legacy
+API, or correct the triangular-number inference.
+
+### 9. Low: pybind narrows the ESS count to `int`
+
+The core count is `size_t`, but `cpp/src/pybind_module.cpp:41` stores it in an
+`int` and `:114` casts explicitly. Large combinatorial result counts can be
+truncated. Keep the count as `size_t` or `uint64_t`; Python integers do not need
+the narrowing.
