+## Open Speed Findings
+
+### 1. Critical for batch output: Arrow and Parquet write one batch per matrix
+
+`python/wrapper_v1/sinks_arrow.py:58-66` creates one-row record batches and
+`python/wrapper_v1/sinks_parquet.py:31-50` creates one-row tables/row groups.
+A local 10,000-result benchmark measured:
 
 ```text
-cpp/src/pybind_module.cpp
-python/wrapper_v1/
+Parquet current: 1.458 s, 14,905,502 bytes
+Parquet batched: 0.024 s,     60,110 bytes
+Arrow current:   0.429 s,  5,840,512 bytes
+Arrow batched:   0.020 s,    492,296 bytes
 ```
+
+Buffer fixed-size row batches before writing. The measured Parquet path was
+about 60x slower and 248x larger; Arrow was about 22x slower and 12x larger.
+
+### 2. `--fullsupport` materializes all supports before checking full support
+
+`cpp/src/fracessa.cpp:49-57` calls `Supports::initialize()` before the direct
+full-support check. For dimension 24 this materialized 16,777,215 masks and
+used about 134 MB in a local benchmark, even if the first requested check could
+return immediately.
+
+Check the full mask before support initialization. Initialize the remaining
+support buckets only if fallback search is necessary.
+
+### 3. Support indices are extracted repeatedly, not once per support
+
+For a support reaching exact validation, indices are extracted in
+`MatrixServer::get_linear_system_dbl`, `find_candidate_dbl`,
+`MatrixServer::get_linear_system_frc`, and `find_candidate_frc`. This contradicts
+the intended "extract once per support" hot-path policy.
+
+Extract support/complement indices in `search_one_support` and pass the fixed
+stack buffers through both matrix construction and candidate checks. Also,
+`cpp/src/findeq.cpp:50-56` builds `full_solution`, but the subsequent loop reads
+only support entries; use `solution(j_pos,0)` directly.
+
+### 4. Exact candidate vectors are built before they are known to be candidates
+
+`cpp/src/findeq.cpp:97-106` fills the full rational vector before outside-row
+validation at `:108-121`, and does so even when candidates and logging are both
+disabled. Stability does not read the vector.
+
+Validate outside rows first. Materialize the full vector only after success and
+only when candidate output or logging needs it.
+
+### 5. Partial copositivity retains an entire reduction history
+
+`cpp/src/checkstab.cpp:94-102` allocates four `r+1` vectors, including every
+intermediate rational matrix. Each iteration only needs the previous matrix,
+the new matrix, and current scalar bitsets/sizes.
+
+Use scalar state plus two rolling matrix buffers. This removes avoidable exact
+matrix copies and heap/object lifetime work.
+
+### 6. LU represents a permutation as a dense rational matrix
+
+`cpp/include/linalg/lu_factor_fraction.hpp:40-43` allocates `m_P` as `n*n`
+fractions. Every solve applies it with an `n*n` rational `addmul` loop
+(`:118-127`), and inverse calls solve once per column. Almost all operations
+multiply by zero.
+
+Store a permutation index array and apply it with `bp[i] = b[perm[i]]`. This
+removes an exact matrix allocation and O(n^3) zero/one rational operations from
+inverse construction.
+
+### 7. Copositivity builds every subset before it can fail early
+
+`cpp/include/linalg/copositive_fraction.hpp:159-167` materializes all nonempty
+subsets grouped by size before checking the first one. Each subset then builds
+a rational submatrix which `LU_Factorization` deep-copies (`:107-124`).
+
+Generate fixed-cardinality masks on demand so a failing small principal
+submatrix stops immediately. Benchmark a move/destructive LU path to avoid the
+second submatrix copy on the common nonsingular path.
+
+### 8. Python `chunk_size` does not batch IPC
+
+`python/wrapper_v1/mp.py:46-55` uses `chunk_size` only to calculate a submission
+window. Every matrix and result is still one queue/pickle operation. For
+millions of short matrices, submit and return actual chunks while each worker
+continues to process one matrix at a time.
+
+Ordered mode also refills based on yielded order (`:188-207`), so an early
+long-running matrix can leave workers idle while later completed results wait
+behind it. Track received work separately from ordered delivery.
+
+### 9. The speed benchmark reports optimistic noise and times candidate work
+
+`python/run_matrices.py:37-45` runs short matrices up to 20 times and reports the
+minimum. It also enables candidate collection for every timed run (`:51-57`),
+although the script is documented as speed-only. Minimum timing systematically
+selects lucky noise, and candidate creation measures optional output work.
+
+Use warmups plus a median (and dispersion), with candidates disabled for core
+speed measurements. If candidate performance matters, benchmark it separately.
+
+### 10. The active speed script still uses the subprocess wrapper
+
+`python/run_matrices.py:22` imports the 316-line legacy `fracessa_py.py` even
+though wrapper v1 provides in-process pybind execution. Migrating removes one
+process launch and text-output parse per matrix invocation and eliminates the
+duplicated, incorrect `Matrix` parser.
+
+## CI And Test Reality
+
+1. Fresh native ARM release build: C++ core and CLI compiled successfully.
+2. Core/CLI CTest suite: 10/10 passed.
+3. Verification CTests: 35/35 passed (33 fast plus IDs 32 and 34).
+4. Native wrapper unittests: 23/23 passed, including multiprocessing tests.
+5. UBSan/warning build compiled; nine non-bitset core tests passed. Bitset tests
+   exposed the zero-input `ctz` undefined behavior described above.
+6. Existing verification data does not contain either critical numerical
+   reproducer, so all baseline tests pass while both real correctness bugs
+   remain.
+7. `.github/workflows/release.yml:3-6` runs only for release tags, not pushes or
+   pull requests to `main`. Broken code can therefore reach `main` before CI.
+8. GitHub is reachable. `main`, `origin/main`, and tag `v0.33` point to
+   `81a6849`. The v0.33 release run built successfully on Linux/macOS but failed
+   `Bitset64Test.FindNextSetBitAtTopBit`; Windows and publication were cancelled.
+   The local top-bit fix is still uncommitted, so GitHub remains red.
+
+Recommended test additions, in order:
+
+1. The two critical numerical matrices above, asserting default and exact
+   mathematical equality.
+2. Scale-invariance property tests over several positive rational factors.
+3. A policy test that `--exact` never emits a `*_dbl` stability decision.
+4. UBSan CI for bitset and parser boundary tests.
+5. Saturated-queue, dead-worker, and long-running-worker multiprocessing tests.
+6. CI on every push/pull request; keep release publication tag-only.
+
